Pattern Card — Deployment-Velocity Shock

LOOKAHEAD-TAINTED — new.

Today’s semiconductor collapse gives us a variable our earlier AI model omitted.

Leading indicator: credible political, regulatory, safety, legal or physical constraint capable of delaying deployment rather than reducing eventual demand.

Mechanism:

Expected\ TAM
\quad unchanged

but

Time\ to\ TAM\uparrow
\Rightarrow PV(TAM)\downarrow.

For capital-intensive suppliers:

PV =
\sum_t
\frac{FCF_t\times P(deployment_t)}
{(1+r)^t}.

Today both terms moved adversely:

P(deployment_{near})\downarrow

and

r\uparrow.

That’s a multiplicative, not merely additive, problem for long-duration AI infrastructure. The chip index’s 5.9% fall versus the S&P’s 0.48% decline is consistent with that mechanism. 

Transfer conditions: capital-intensive technology whose valuation assumes rapid rollout.

False friend: rhetoric without policy/customer-capex consequences.

Invalidation: hyperscaler capex, chip orders and deployment schedules continue accelerating despite slowdown rhetoric.

Empirical score: 1 hindsight episode, 0 prospective validations.

We now watch actual orders and capex. Headlines don’t graduate the pattern.

